## Customize flags given to the VM: https://www.erlang.org/doc/man/erl.html
## -mode/-name/-sname/-setcookie are configured via env vars, do not set them here

## Expert review 2026-07-24 #2. Before this file existed the release shipped pure ERTS defaults —
## no rel/, no vm.args, no ERL_FLAGS anywhere in config/, deploy/, or the Dockerfiles. Every value
## below was therefore load-bearing at its default, and three of them are sized for a general-
## purpose BEAM app rather than for fathom's shape (a SQLite-per-shard database server holding tens
## of thousands of connections per node).
##
## These are capacity and scheduling knobs: none of them change data paths, durability guarantees,
## or ordering. They are documented individually because they should be A/B'd individually on the
## chaos rig (chaos.sh tpc-fleet / served-data) rather than accepted as a block.

## --- Dirty IO schedulers -----------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE IMPORTANT ONE. Every exqlite NIF is ERL_NIF_DIRTY_JOB_IO_BOUND (open, prepare, step,
## multi_step, columns, changes, last_insert_rowid, ...), so ALL SQLite work on the node — every
## tenant, every shard — runs on this thread pool. The ERTS default is 10, independent of core
## count, which makes 10 the node-wide ceiling on concurrent SQLite operations.
##
## Two fathom properties make those threads BLOCK rather than just execute:
##   * PRAGMA synchronous=FULL fsyncs the WAL inside multi_step, so a commit pins a thread for the
##     fsync duration (~0.1 ms local NVMe, ~0.5-1 ms on cloud block storage).
##   * A contended write parks in the busy handler for up to the busy timeout.
## A read query costs >=4 dirty-IO dispatches, so at the measured ~27k q/s/node that is ~135k
## dirty-IO jobs/s across (by default) ten threads.
##
## The "clean CPU plateau" reported in docs/reviews/tpc-fleet-elixir-driver-2026-07-24.md has never
## been tested against this hypothesis. Sweep 10 / 64 / 256 and watch aggregate tps; a standing
## non-zero :erlang.system_info(:dirty_io_run_queue) during a run is the direct proof.
##
## Dirty-IO threads sleep when idle; the cost is thread stacks (~1 MB RSS per 32 threads).
## Do NOT raise +SDcpu — it defaults to schedulers_online, which is correct here.
+SDio 64

## --- Process and port tables -------------------------------------------------------------------
## Production materializes 3-4 processes and 1 port per SERVED shard (Bandit connection handler,
## Filo.Stream, the Fathom.Shard coordinator, plus a watchdog when :query_timeout_ms is set).
## chaos.sh served-data reached 105k shards held (~35k/node) and extrapolates to ~50k/node.
##
## The defaults are +P 262144 and +Q 65536. deploy/chaos/docker-compose.yml already raises nofile to
## 65536 specifically so fds stop binding — but +Q caps ports regardless of nofile, so that fd
## headroom was unreachable past ~65k concurrent connections.
##
## Neither limit degrades gracefully: +P exhaustion makes spawn throw system_limit (a supervisor
## start failure, i.e. refused checkouts) and +Q exhaustion makes gen_tcp:accept return
## {:error, :system_limit} (the listener stops accepting). Both are hard availability cliffs, and
## they sat an order of magnitude below the fd limit the compose file was raised to reach.
##
## Cost is the preallocated pointer tables: ~8 MB for +P, ~2 MB for +Q. Trivial against the ~6 GB
## a served-data node already uses.
+P 1048576
+Q 262144

## --- IO poll threads ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Defaults are +IOp 1 / +IOt 1: ONE OS thread dispatching socket readiness for every socket on the
## node — Hrana connections, the health listener, the Phoenix endpoint, and every Finch connection
## to S3. ThousandIsland's sockets are {active, false}, which does not bypass the pollset.
##
## chaos.sh served measured 30k held connections (10k/node) and served-data was pushed to 105k, so
## the documented working set is two orders of magnitude above what one poll thread is provisioned
## for. UNMEASURED here — no number is being claimed. Attribute it with :msacc (start/print via
## bin/fathom rpc), which reports time by thread type including `poll`.
+IOt 4
+IOp 2

## --- Scheduler busy-wait (NOT enabled; needs measurement) --------------------------------------
## `+sbwt none +sbwtdcpu none` stops schedulers spinning before they sleep, which is a standard win
## for IO-bound BEAM services and would matter on a co-tenanted VM (the chaos rig is exactly that).
## Left commented deliberately: unlike the knobs above it can REDUCE throughput on latency-sensitive
## workloads by adding wake-up latency, and there is no way to tell which way it goes for fathom
## without running it on the rig. Enable only behind an A/B.
##+sbwt none
##+sbwtdcpu none

## Enable deployment without epmd
## (requires changing both vm.args and remote.vm.args)
##-start_epmd false -erl_epmd_port 6789
